ered with G4, which lost 4.0 points and moved the lead from +2.0 to -2.0. The engine's choice was D7, away from the H4 stone."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "White's H5 lost 6.8 points, the 12k's worst move, and it gave back a lead of +4.8 for Black. White's stones at H4, H5 and H6 then formed a wall along the right edge."}, {"turn": 13, "text": "Opus 5.5's H3 was the worst move of the game, 8.4 points lost, turning +4.8 into -3.5. The lead was handed back right after White's slip, and Black never got ahead again."}, {"turn": 21, "text": "H8 lost 5.9 points and took the lead from -9.1 to -14.9. Black's stones at G8 and H8 sat in the top right above White's wall, short of support, and White's F8 followed next."}, {"turn": 37, "text": "By B8 Black was down -23.1, and the move lost 4.3 more, to -27.5. The final board shows Black stones along the B and C files in the top left, but White held the middle and the lead kept growing until resignation."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py flood-fills each group on a pasted board and prints its stones and liberty count, and count.py (run with dead stones removed) counts stones and enclosed territory for the current position. Neither one proposes, ranks or looks ahead at moves, so both only inspect the current position.", "stop": false}}